WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  the pope's welcome was warm but this  visit was about repairing deep wounds  at the site of a former catholic  residential school where for decades  indigenous children were separated from  their parents abused neglected  finally the words canada's indigenous  community has sought for generations  i am here  because the first step of my penitential  pilgrimage among you  is that of again asking forgiveness  of telling you once more  that i am deeply sorry  at least 150 000 indigenous children  were forced into church-run schools in  canada malnutrition was rife children  were physically and sexually abused  the last of these institutions only  closed in the 1990s  and many survivors came to hear the  apology in person  our languages  were suppressed  our culture  taken from us  and our spirituality  denigrated  our spirituality  denigrated  you have heard the devastation that  followed from the way our families were  torn apart  hundreds of unmarked graves have been  found near former homes where children  died at rates several times above the  national average  the pope's apology won't heal their  losses and trauma but it's the  acknowledgment they've long sought that  the church was complicit in their abuse  sally lockwood sky news washington

